" (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.01598v1)\nciteturn2732view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.01598v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 487\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: Securing generative artificial intelligence with parallel magnetic tunnel junction true\nL2@P0: randomness\nL3@P0: Youwei Bao, Shuhan Yang, and Hyunsoo Yang*\nL4@P0: Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, National University of Singapore,\nL5@P0: Singapore 117583, Singapore\nL6@P0: E-mail: eleyang@nus.edu.sg\nL7@P0: Deterministic pseudo random number generators (PRNGs) used in generative artificial\nL8@P0: intelligence (GAI) models produce predictable patterns vulnerable to exploitation by attackers.\nL9@P0: Conventional defenses against the vulnerabilities often come with significant energy and\nL10@P0: latency overhead. Here, we embed hardware-generated true random bits from spin-transfer\nL11@P0: torque magnetic tunnel junctions (STT-MTJs) to address the challenges. A highly parallel,\nL12@P0: FPGA-assisted prototype computing system delivers megabit-per-second true random numbers,\nL13@P0: passing NIST randomness tests after in-situ operations with minimal overhead. Integrating the\nL14@P0: hardware random bits into a generative adversarial network (GAN) trained on CIFAR-10\nL15@P0: reduces insecure outputs by up to 18.6 times compared to the low-quality random number\nL16@P0: generators (RNG) baseline. With nanosecond switching speed, high energy efficiency, and\nL17@P0: established scalability, our STT-MTJ-based system holds the potential to scale beyond 106\nL18@P0: parallel cells, achieving gigabit-per-second throughput suitable for large language model\nL19@P0: sampling. This advancement highlights spintronic RNGs as practical security components for\nL20@P0: next-generation GAI systems.\nL21@P0: 1. Introduction\nL22@P0: Generative artificial intelligence (GAI), which uses machine learning models to generate\nL23@P0: text,[1] images,[2] music,[3] videos,[4] and complex simulations,[5] has transformed industries and\nL24@P0: reshaped daily life. It has achieved remarkable commercial success across various applications,\nL25@P0: including ChatGPT, Midjourney, and Sora. However, the rapid advancements outpace the\nL26@P0: development of security measures. Previous studies[6] documented numerous attacks, and more\nL27@P0: are expected in real-world scenarios. Research indicates that attackers can bypass built-in\nL28@P0: security measures in models like GPT-4 without direct access to the internal model structure.\nL29@P0-1: Simply by interacting with the model application programming interface (API) and fine-tuning 2\nL30@P1: it with as few as 15 harmful examples, attackers can make the model generate malicious\nL31@P1: content.[7] A common aspect of these exploits is the deterministic nature of pseudo random\nL32@P1: number generators (PRNGs),[8] which supply latent codes, diffusion noise, or token sampling.\nL33@P1: Since the PRNG produces identical sequences for a given seed, the attacker can iteratively\nL34@P1: query the model, analyze the relationship between inputs and outputs, estimate the underlying\nL35@P1: gradients, and gradually direct the model to generate harmful content.[6a] Adversarial training[9]\nL36@P1: is a conventional defense strategy for traditional machine learning models. It expands datasets\nL37@P1: with adversarial examples and optimizes the entire network. By learning to handle challenging\nL38@P1: adversarial examples correctly, models become more robust against future attacks. However,\nL39@P1: generating adversarial examples and training the models using them leads to overall durations\nL40@P1: 3 to 30 times longer,[10] which substantially increases energy consumption and costs, making\nL41@P1: the strategy impractical for large-scale GAI models.\nL42@P1: Instead of training models to handle harmful examples, a more cost-effective strategy is to\nL43@P1: reduce vulnerability during inference.[11] As illustrated in Figure 1a, during black-box attacks,\nL44@P1: attackers systematically probe the model by varying user inputs, such as seed values, and\nL45@P1: observing the outputs. This process enables attackers to understand how user inputs correspond\nL46@P1: to specific outputs, as consistent patterns emerge. For example, certain seeds consistently yield\nL47@P1: specific features, providing attackers insights into confidential training data or enabling them\nL48@P1: to generate specific harmful outputs. The deterministic nature creates a fixed dependency chain\nL49@P1: from user input (e.g., seed) through the PRNG-generated random sequence (latent code) to the\nL50@P1: model output (e.g., generated image). Moreover, if the PRNG demonstrates biases that narrow\nL51@P1: the output range, attackers can focus on a narrower subset, amplifying their attack success\nL52@P1: rate.\nL53@P1: [12] In contrast, the inherent unpredictability of true random number generators (TRNGs)\nL54@P1: disrupts the fixed dependency chain, preventing attackers from consistently crafting inputs that\nL55@P1: yield predictable patterns.\nL56@P1: Among TRNGs,[13] spin-transfer torque magnetic tunnel junctions (STT-MTJs) stand out\nL57@P1: due to their nanosecond switching speeds enabling mega to giga bit per second throughput,[14]\nL58@P1: sub-picojoule energy per bit,[15] and mature fabrication processes with demonstrated giga-size\nL59@P1: array densities.[16] Despite promising progress highlighting these MTJ advantages, research\nL60@P1: remains largely device-centric,[17] without connecting to system-level AI performance.[18] To\nL61@P1: bridge the gap, we implement a 16-MTJ system and interface with a field-programmable gate\nL62@P1: array (FPGA). Our system architecture enables parallel individual access to all MTJ cells.\nL63@P1: Therefore, the bit-per-second, which serves as the key performance metric, scales linearly with\nL64@P1-2: the number of MTJ cells that can be accessed in parallel. Due to fluctuations of environmental 3\nL65@P2: and measurement conditions, the raw MTJ outputs exhibit reduced randomness quality. Thus,\nL66@P2: we implement two post-processing schemes: a lightweight bitwise XOR between raw bits,[19]\nL67@P2: and a standard Toeplitz hash meeting cryptographic randomness standard.[17d, 20] Both processed\nL68@P2: streams pass NIST SP 800-22 tests[8] with 100% success. In addition, we embed the TRNG into\nL69@P2: the latent-code generator of a deep generative adversarial network (GAN) trained on CIFAR\u000210. Inference using enhanced MTJ-based latent codes reduces low-diversity (biased insecure)\nL70@P2: image clusters by up to 18.6× across 10,000 generated images per RNG. Although our\nL71@P2: experimental focus is a GAN, the same entropy source naturally applies to other GAI workflows.\nL72@P2: On edge devices, the high throughput and low-power MTJ entropy facilitates on-device\nL73@P2: generation without cloud exposure, aligning with AI safety frameworks.[21] Our work\nL74@P2: establishes STT-MTJ TRNGs as practical, high performance, and energy-efficient building\nL75@P2: blocks for secure and responsible GAI.\nL76@P2: 2. Results and Discussion\nL77@P2: 2.1. Constructing the MTJ-based computing system\nL78@P2: An STT-MTJ consists of two ferromagnetic layers separated by a thin insulating barrier.\nL79@P2: One layer has a fixed magnetization, while the other, known as the free layer, can switch its\nL80@P2: magnetic orientation. The relative alignment of the ferromagnetic layers determines the\nL81@P2: resistance of the MTJ, resulting in two stable states: parallel (P), with a low resistance, and\nL82@P2: antiparallel (AP), with a high resistance, representing binary values 1 and 0. STT is used to\nL83@P2: switch the MTJ between the two states by applying a current that manipulates the magnetic\nL84@P2: moment of the free layer. In conventional memory applications, controlled current pulses are\nL85@P2: used to deterministically set the MTJ into a specific state. However, by carefully tuning the\nL86@P2: amplitude and duration of the current pulses, the MTJ can be driven into a metastable regime\nL87@P2: where thermal fluctuations determine the final state.[13b, 17a] As shown in Figure 1b, we tune the\nL88@P2: switching probability by varying the amplitude of voltage pulses Vdd (the pulse width is fixed\nL89@P2: at 5 µs). The symbols depict a sigmoid-like switching probability response to different input\nL90@P2: amplitudes from 16 individual MTJs. The switching probability is calculated by averaging the\nL91@P2: MTJ outputs over 1,000 input pulses. The stochastic switching behavior enables the MTJ to\nL92@P2: function as a TRNG, as the output is inherently unpredictable and governed by thermal noise.\nL93@P2: Based on this mechanism, we construct a system that consists of 16 MTJs with a peripheral\nL94@P2: circuit and an FPGA, as illustrated in Figure 1c. A 16-channel digital-to-analog converter (DAC)\nL95@P2: supplies 16 analog input voltages to control the switching probability of each individual MTJ,\nL96@P2-3: while a 16-channel analog-to-digital converter (ADC) samples the resulting state of each MTJ 4\nL97@P3: (binarized using a predefined threshold) after each input pulse. The setup enables the parallel\nL98@P3: usage of all 16 MTJ cells, with the output raw bits transmit to and store in the FPGA. For our\nL99@P3: generation task, we set the switching probability of all computing cells to ~50%, as shown in\nL100@P3: Figure 1d. Further details on the development and characterization of our computing system\nL101@P3: are provided in the Experimental Section. In practice, the ADC used in our prototype could be\nL102@P3: replaced by read sense amplifiers (RSAs),[22] which are well established in magnetic random\u0002access memory technology and offer significantly lower energy costs and smaller footprints.\nL103@P3: Thus, by utilizing the intrinsic stochastic switching behavior of MTJ devices, we can generate\nL104@P3: random numbers, which can be further optimized for resource-efficient operation.\nL105@P3: 2.2. Statistical property analysis of RNGs\nL106@P3: After obtaining the raw random bit sequence, we verify the functionality of our MTJ as an\nL107@P3: RNG. Each 32-bit binary output is converted to its decimal equivalent, and a histogram based\nL108@P3: on 107 bits is shown in Figure 2a. Despite fine tuning, the raw data are clearly not uniformly\nL109@P3: distributed. As the thermal noise such as device heating is unlikely to remain constant\nL110@P3: throughout the experiment, and even slight variations can skew the distribution of output bits\nL111@P3: away from uniformity. Moreover, assuming complete independence between raw output bits\nL112@P3: would be unrealistic. Imperfections in the experimental setup, such as noisy read circuits, may\nL113@P3: introduce correlations, further degrading the quality of randomness. To address these issues, we\nL114@P3: apply two established post-processing techniques (Section S1, Supporting Information). The\nL115@P3: first is a lightweight scheme with negligible energy overhead, where we use intrinsic memory\nL116@P3: operations to perform a bitwise XOR across three raw bits (MTJ with XOR). The second is a\nL117@P3: standard extraction scheme using Toeplitz hashing (MTJ extraction), a member of the universal\nL118@P3: hashing family that offers provably secure randomness extraction. To reduce the computational\nL119@P3: cost of Toeplitz hashing, we adopt the fast Fourier transform (FFT). The 32-bit binary outputs\nL120@P3: from the post-processed random sequence are again converted to decimal form, and the\nL121@P3: resulting histograms are shown in Figure 2b,c. These distributions exhibit visually satisfactory\nL122@P3: uniformity. For comparison, we also implement a linear feedback shift register (LFSR), a\nL123@P3: commonly used resource-efficient PRNG. Its output histogram, shown in Figure 2d,\nL124@P3: demonstrates good uniformity.\nL125@P3: Next, we evaluate all four RNGs using the NIST SP 800-22 Statistical Test Suite (NIST\nL126@P3: tests),[8] a standard consisting of 15 tests designed to assess the statistical quality of random\nL127@P3: bitstreams. Following the recommended sequence length, we test 5.5×107 bits of random data\nL128@P3-4: for each RNG. As summarized in Table 1, both MTJ-based TRNG schemes (MTJ with XOR 5\nL129@P4: and MTJ extraction) pass all tests, demonstrating high-quality randomness and strong security\nL130@P4: properties. Moreover, the uniformity-of-p-values of the two schemes cover the full [0, 1] range:\nL131@P4: from 0.0002 to 0.9943 for MTJ with XOR, and from 0.0006 to 0.9879 for MTJ extraction. Over\nL132@P4: 96% of the values fall within the [0.1, 0.9] range, with only 7 and 4 sub-tests below 0.01,\nL133@P4: respectively, and none below 0.0001. This indicates a uniform distribution, reflecting robust\nL134@P4: randomness rather than a small number of lucky passes. In contrast, the MTJ raw data and the\nL135@P4: LFSR fail to pass all the tests. As discussed earlier, the failure of the raw MTJ output is expected\nL136@P4: due to its non-uniform distribution and bit correlations. Although the LFSR output appears\nL137@P4: visually uniform, it has fundamental limitations.\nL138@P4: [23] LFSRs generate sequences through linear\nL139@P4: operations on previous bits, resulting in low linear complexity. This causes failures in the linear\nL140@P4: complexity test. Moreover, structured dependencies among bits can reduce the rank of disjoint\nL141@P4: sub-matrices constructed during the rank test, leading to failure in this test as well. These\nL142@P4: limitations degrade randomness quality and thus compromise security.\nL143@P4: Notably, advanced general-purpose PRNGs, such as Xoroshiro128+, may pass all NIST\nL144@P4: tests. However, their deterministic nature opens the door to black-box attacks at the application\nL145@P4: level. As a secure alternative, cryptographically secure PRNGs (CSPRNGs) are designed to\nL146@P4: generate highly unpredictable numbers. However, a CSPRNG is significantly more complex\nL147@P4: and resource-intensive than general-purpose PRNGs. It requires additional components such as\nL148@P4: ciphers, cryptographic hash functions, runtime tests, and robust seeding mechanisms that rely\nL149@P4: on a true random seed to initiate a secure sequence.[24] In contrast, our enhanced MTJ-based\nL150@P4: TRNGs not only demonstrate strong statistical properties by passing all NIST tests but also\nL151@P4: produce genuinely unpredictable outputs. In particular, our lightweight scheme has negligible\nL152@P4: energy overhead while significantly improving randomness, which makes it a promising and\nL153@P4: resource-efficient solution for AI security applications.\nL154@P4: 2.3. Image generation with MTJ-based TRNGs\nL155@P4: We further implement a GAN that integrates our hardware RNGs. Compared to their early\nL156@P4: predecessors,[25] recent GANs have significantly advanced in their ability to generate realistic\nL157@P4: and diverse images, benefiting from improvements in architectural design and training\nL158@P4: strategies.[26] However, the fundamental structure of GANs remains largely unchanged,\nL159@P4: consisting of two main components: a generator and a discriminator, as illustrated in Figure 3a.\nL160@P4: During image generation, GANs use a vector of random numbers (latent code) as input to seed\nL161@P4-5: stochastic image synthesis. The generator progressively transforms the latent code into human\u0002interpretable images through multiple intermediate layers. Apart from the latent code, all other 6\nL162@P5: operations within the model are deterministic. Figure 3a shows an example of a dog image\nL163@P5: generated from the initial latent code, through intermediate-layer representations, to the final\nL164@P5: output. By varying the latent code, the GAN produces diverse images. The discriminator\nL165@P5: performs two tasks: class prediction, to identify the class label of the input image using ground\nL166@P5: truth labels from datasets like CIFAR-10,[27] and adversarial prediction, to determine whether\nL167@P5: the image is real (from dataset) or generated by the model. During training, the discriminator\nL168@P5: predictions are iteratively compared against their respective targets, and backpropagation is\nL169@P5: used to update the weights of both the generator and the discriminator (Figure 3a). Specifically,\nL170@P5: we implement an autoencoder-based discriminator and adopt a training strategy designed to\nL171@P5: enhance image quality (Table S1 and Section S2, Supporting Information). The model is pre\u0002trained on a GPU and demonstrates superior performance compared to classical GANs.[26a, 28]\nL172@P5: Next, we replace the algorithmic RNG with our hardware-based RNGs during inference\nL173@P5: for image generation. The latent code for each generated image is a vector of length 110,\nL174@P5: comprising 100 random numbers in the range [-1, 1], along with an additional 10 numbers\nL175@P5: encoding class information. To generate each random number in the latent code vector, we take\nL176@P5: a full 32-bit word from our random bit sequence, producing a binarized sequence. First, we\nL177@P5: convert the binary value to decimal, and then we normalize it to the [-1, 1] range. This\nL178@P5: normalized value is then fed into the GAN via a Python interface in single-precision floating\u0002point format. Producing 100 random numbers consumes a total of 3,200 bits to generate a single\nL179@P5: image. In Figure 3b, we illustrate a latent code matrix for generating a batch of 100 images.\nL180@P5: Subsequently, we generate 10,000 images (each 64×64 pixels) using all our hardware-based\nL181@P5: RNGs.\nL182@P5: To evaluate model performance, we calculate the Inception score[29] (IS) for the generated\nL183@P5: images, a widely adopted metric for assessing generative models. The IS evaluates both the\nL184@P5: objectness (i.e., each image is classified with high certainty) and the class diversity (i.e., the\nL185@P5: classifier used for evaluation identifies a wide variety of classes among images) of generated\nL186@P5: images. The results, shown in Figure 3c, demonstrate that our model achieves an IS as high as\nL187@P5: 10.28 (the higher, the better). This score closely approaches the performance of recent state-of\u0002the-art models[30] and is not far below the IS of the CIFAR-10 training dataset itself (11.24),\nL188@P5: which typically represents an upper boundary for models trained on the same dataset. Among\nL189@P5: all tested hardware RNGs, we observe similar levels of IS. However, evaluating generative\nL190@P5: models quantitatively remains challenging. Although the IS is a popular metric, it may overlook\nL191@P5: certain aspects.[26a] Therefore, additional metrics, such as learned perceptual image patch\nL192@P5-6: similarity (LPIPS), are analyzed in the following section. Figure 3d provides examples of 7\nL193@P6: images generated using our MTJ with XOR TRNG, each clearly matching its corresponding\nL194@P6: class. Details of the IS evaluation and additional image results for each RNG are in Figure S1\nL195@P6: and Section S3, Supporting Information.\nL196@P6: 2.4. System-level analysis of RNGs\nL197@P6: By carefully observing the generated images, we notice repetitive patterns in the results\nL198@P6: produced by low-quality RNGs (MTJ raw data and LFSR). Examples of visually similar image\nL199@P6: pairs are presented in Figure 4a. Intuitively, we perceive similarities within each pair,\nL200@P6: particularly regarding color and the shape of main objects (such as a deer or a car in the\nL201@P6: examples provided). However, these pairs are subtly different, indicating correlation rather than\nL202@P6: exact duplication. To better understand the observed correlations, we extract feature maps from\nL203@P6: similar image pairs (see details in Section S4, Supporting Information). Figure 4a shows low\u0002level feature maps, which capture basic image attributes that are intuitively interpretable by\nL204@P6: humans, while Figure 4b displays high-level feature maps, capturing more abstract patterns\nL205@P6: meaningful to AI systems. As expected, the low-level features within each image pair closely\nL206@P6: resemble each other, aligning with human perception. Interestingly, high-level features also\nL207@P6: exhibit similar patterns, with comparable values appearing at similar spatial locations. Our\nL208@P6: observations indicate the presence of correlations within the latent space, which are directly\nL209@P6: influenced by the quality of the underlying RNG. Given that the IS primarily measures class\nL210@P6: diversity rather than intra-class diversity,[31] we further investigate diversity among images\nL211@P6: generated within the same class. This is important since biased model outputs can directly\nL212@P6: compromise security.\nL213@P6: To systematically evaluate image diversity, we apply t-distributed stochastic neighbour\nL214@P6: embedding[32] (t-SNE), a statistical method for visualizing high-dimensional data (i.e., 64×64\nL215@P6: pixel images) by mapping them into a lower-dimensional (typically two-dimensional) space. t\u0002SNE preserves local relationships between the data; points close in the original high\u0002dimensional space remain nearby after dimensionality reduction, while distant points remain\nL216@P6: separated. Figure 4c displays t-SNE plots from images generated by a low-quality RNG (LFSR,\nL217@P6: left panel) and a high-quality RNG (MTJ with XOR, right panel), where each generated image\nL218@P6: corresponds to a single data point (10,000 points per plot). Smaller distances between points\nL219@P6: indicate greater similarity. Images generated by the LFSR form tightly clustered patterns,\nL220@P6: indicating lower diversity, whereas those from the MTJ with XOR RNG show more scattered\nL221@P6: and loosely structured distributions, suggesting higher intra-class diversity. Details of the t-SNE\nL222@P6-7: are included in Section S5 and Figure S2 of Supporting Information. 8\nL223@P7: Furthermore, we use LPIPS[33] to quantitatively measure similarity between image pairs.\nL224@P7: LPIPS scores range from 0 to 1, with lower scores indicating greater similarity; we consider\nL225@P7: image pairs with scores below 0.3 to be perceptually similar (and thus correlated and lower\u0002quality). We randomly divide 1,000 generated images from the same class into two groups,\nL226@P7: compute pairwise LPIPS scores, and count the number of similar image pairs (Sections S6 and\nL227@P7: S7, Supporting Information; Figure S3 to S5). As illustrated in Figure 4d, our MTJ with XOR\nL228@P7: RNG achieves performance comparable to the established cybersecurity-grade RNG[17d] (MTJ\nL229@P7: extraction). A clear performance gap emerges between low-quality and high-quality RNGs,\nL230@P7: notably between MTJ raw data and its improved counterparts. Specifically, for class 6, the\nL231@P7: improved MTJ RNGs achieve an 18.6 times improvement over the MTJ raw data RNG.\nL232@P7: Performance differences vary across image classes; for instance, classes 3 and 5 exhibit small\nL233@P7: differences between the LFSR and our improved MTJ RNGs. We interpret the observed latent\u0002space correlations as more complex than the statistical correlations typically detected by\nL234@P7: standard NIST tests, such as the linear complexity test or rank test. Previous studies[34] suggest\nL235@P7: that the latent codes in GANs behave analogously to biological genes, where each dimension\nL236@P7: controls specific characteristics of the generated images. These \"genes\" interact in a complex,\nL237@P7: non-uniform, and entangled manner. Although an LFSR provides good bit-level uniformity, its\nL238@P7: inherently linear structure is insufficient for eliminating the deeper, more subtle correlations\nL239@P7: within the latent space. Consequently, at the application level, an LFSR produces more biased\nL240@P7: image outputs compared to high-quality TRNGs. These biases are also task-specific and model\u0002dependent, resulting in variability across different image classes. Given that even minor biases\nL241@P7: can significantly impact security, it is necessary to integrate GAI with high-quality RNGs.\nL242@P7: 3. Conclusion\nL243@P7: Compared to general-purpose PRNGs, our improved MTJ TRNGs offer security\nL244@P7: advantages. General-purpose PRNGs are inherently deterministic and thus, even those with\nL245@P7: good statistical properties remain vulnerable to attacks. Moreover, achieving good statistical\nL246@P7: properties typically requires careful design and implementation. As demonstrated in Figure S6\nL247@P7: and Section S7, Supporting Information, intentionally poorly designed LFSRs clearly show\nL248@P7: how easily statistical properties can be compromised. On the other hand, CSPRNGs offer secure\nL249@P7: solutions but introduce significant energy overhead. Typical CSPRNG implementations\nL250@P7: consume tens to hundreds of nanojoules per random bit,\nL251@P7: [24] which is over 105 times more than\nL252@P7: the ~1 pJ per random bit[15] estimated for our lightweight scheme when scaled to systems with\nL253@P7-8: 106 devices (Figure S7 and S8, Table S2 to S4, and Section S8, Supporting Information). The 9\nL254@P8: energy efficiency is especially important for constrained IoT devices powered by small batteries,\nL255@P8: where energy depletion attacks may even occur.[24]\nL256@P8: In summary, we experimentally implement a hardware RNG system based on MTJs with\nL257@P8: CMOS for random sequence generation. We explore two enhancement schemes to improve\nL258@P8: statistical properties: a lightweight scheme using in-situ operations and a standard scheme\nL259@P8: applying Toeplitz hashing. These improvements enable our MTJ-based TRNGs to ensure\nL260@P8: security at both the device and application levels. Integrating the TRNGs into GAI greatly\nL261@P8: improves security, particularly against black-box attacks. By combining our TRNGs with\nL262@P8: complementary security strategies, such as robust access controls, we believe it can benefit GAI\nL263@P8: security more broadly. Furthermore, our lightweight RNG scheme is well-suited for IoT\nL264@P8: applications, offering high-quality true randomness with minimal energy overhead. This\nL265@P8: enhancement scheme could also be extended to other TRNG technologies.\nL266@P8: 4. Experimental Section/Methods\nL267@P8: Sample Growth and Device Fabrication: Thin-film samples with the structure of\nL268@P8: substrate/[W (3)/Ru (10)]2/W (3)/Pt (3)/Co (0.25)/Pt (0.2)/[Co (0.25)/Pt (0.5)]5/Co (0.6)/Ru\nL269@P8: (0.85)/Co (0.6)/Pt (0.2)/Co (0.3)/Pt (0.2)/Co (0.5)/W (0.3)/CoFeB (0.9)/MgO (0.85)/CoFeB\nL270@P8: (1.2)/W (0.4)/CoFeB (0.8)/MgO (0.7)/Ta (3)/Ru (7)/Ta (5) are deposited on Si substrates with\nL271@P8: a 300 nm thermal oxide layer via direct current (DC) magnetron sputtering for metallic layers\nL272@P8: and radio frequency (RF) magnetron sputtering for the MgO layers. Deposition is conducted at\nL273@P8: room temperature with a base pressure below 2×10-8 Torr. All thickness values given in\nL274@P8: parentheses are in nanometers. To fabricate the MTJs, bottom electrode structures 10 µm wide\nL275@P8: are initially patterned using photolithography and Ar ion milling. MTJ pillars with diameters of\nL276@P8: ~80 nm are patterned via electron-beam lithography. After ion milling, an encapsulation layer\nL277@P8: of Si3N4 is deposited in-situ without breaking vacuum using RF magnetron sputtering. Finally,\nL278@P8: top electrode structures measuring 10 µm in width are patterned using photolithography, and\nL279@P8: the top electrode layers composed of Ta (5 nm)/Cu (40 nm) are deposited via DC magnetron\nL280@P8: sputtering.\nL281@P8: Printed Circuit Board Design: A 16-channel DAC (AD5767) is used to generate bipolar\nL282@P8: analog input voltages (Vdd) for the 16 MTJs, providing individual control for each MTJ. A 16-\nL283@P8: channel ADC (MAX11131), along with fixed-value sense resistors, samples the outputs from\nL284@P8: each MTJ. The sampled raw data are then sent to an FPGA (VCU118) for storage. The polarity\nL285@P8: of the Vdd voltage determines the current direction through each MTJ, enabling reset and perturb\nL286@P8-9: operations. By adjusting the amplitude of Vdd, the current amplitude flowing through each MTJ 10\nL287@P9: can also be tuned. Communication between the DAC, ADC, and FPGA is facilitated via the\nL288@P9: serial peripheral interface (SPI) protocol. The FPGA is programmed and controlled using\nL289@P9: Verilog. During our experiments, the system operates at a main frequency of 100 kHz. Given\nL290@P9: the parallelism of 16 MTJs, the system achieves a sampling rate of 1.6 million random bits per\nL291@P9: second.\nL292@P9: MTJ-based System Characterization: To generate random bits using STT-MTJ computing\nL293@P9: cells, we first apply a fixed negative Vdd voltage (reset pulse) to deterministically set each MTJ\nL294@P9: to the AP state. Subsequently, we apply a positive Vdd voltage (perturb pulse) to induce\nL295@P9: stochastic switching from the AP state to the P state. The switching probability is controlled by\nL296@P9: adjusting the amplitude of the positive perturb pulse. In our experiments, we aim for a 50%\nL297@P9: switching probability; hence, the perturb pulse amplitude is fine-tuned and fixed at a specific\nL298@P9: value for each MTJ. The ADC then samples the output voltage (Vout) from each MTJ, and the\nL299@P9: sampled values are sent to the FPGA. The FPGA compares each sampled voltage with a pre\u0002characterized threshold voltage (Vth). If Vout exceeds Vth, the bit is stored as ‘1’; otherwise, it is\nL300@P9: stored as ‘0’. Due to the non-volatile nature of MTJ devices, a reset pulse is required before\nL301@P9: generating each random bit. By simultaneously controlling 16 MTJs in parallel using the FPGA,\nL302@P9: we acquire 16 bits at one cycle (reset-perturb). In total, a raw random bit sequence consisting\nL303@P9-10: of 108 bits is recorded.11\nL304@P10: References\nL305@P10: [1] OpenAI, Gpt-4 technical report, arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.08774, 2023.\nL306@P10: [2] J. Betker, G. Goh, L. Jing, T. Brooks, J. Wang, L. Li, L. Ouyang, J. Zhuang, J. Lee, Y.\nL307@P10: Guo, Improving image generation with better captions, Computer Science 2023, 2, 8.\nL308@P10: [3] J. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, A. Défossez,\nL309@P10: Simple and controllable music generation, Adv. Neural Inf. Process. Syst. 2023, 36,\nL310@P10: 47704.\nL311@P10: [4] OpenAI, Video generation models as world simulators, OpenAI Blog 2024, 1, 1.\nL312@P10: [5] H. Gao, S. Kaltenbach, P. Koumoutsakos, Generative learning for forecasting the\nL313@P10: dynamics of high-dimensional complex systems, Nat. Commun. 2024, 15, 8904.\nL314@P10: [6] a)H. Sun, T. Zhu, Z. Zhang, D. Jin, P. Xiong, W. Zhou, Adversarial attacks against\nL315@P10: deep generative models on data: A survey, IEEE Trans. Knowl. Data Eng. 2021, 35,\n"
